Your password has not been changed.

A password may not contain a space or a tab.

The reason is fml's command interface rather than anything about the
password.  Commands arrive as a line and are split on whitespace, on
both sides of this: setting a password reads the words after the
address as separate arguments, and presenting one to log in reads only
the first word after the command.  A password with a space in it could
be set, and could then never be used to log in again.

Refusing it is the only version of this that does not lock you out of
your own list.  NIST SP 800-63B asks that a verifier accept the space
character; fml does not, and this is the limitation being stated rather
than a preference.

Everything else is accepted, including characters outside ASCII, and
there is no limit on length.  A command is now read as the character
encoding its message declares, so a password set from a message written
in UTF-8 is the same password when presented from one written in
ISO-2022-JP, EUC-JP or Shift_JIS.

If you were joining words to make a long password, join them with
something that is not a space.

  quiet-lamp-orbit-tuesday
  quiet.lamp.orbit.tuesday
  quiet7lamp7orbit7tuesday
